# Watermelon — the picked fruit, not the plant.
#
# `art/wam/crops/watermelon_4.wam` already models a melon and this file does not reuse it,
# for the reason `squash.wam` sets out: that one is a fruit lying in a 2x2 sprawl of leaves
# read from two metres up, and this one is held at arm's length in a fist and painted into a
# 32-pixel slot. Handing the crop model to the inventory would put four leaves and a mound
# of stem in the slot and the melon would be the small green thing in the middle of them.
#
# **It stands on its end, and that is a rendering constraint rather than a choice.**
# `ItemModels.js` normalises every model by its Y extent alone and closes the fist somewhere
# along Y, so a melon authored lying down — which is how a melon actually lies — would be
# scaled by its own thickness and gripped across its side. `export_items.py` has a `LYING`
# map that stands such models up on export, but that is for bars and loaves whose long axis
# is unambiguous; a melon is only slightly longer than it is round, and the honest fix is to
# author the axis the renderer already expects. So: an oblong melon on its end, which is
# also how a greengrocer stacks them.
#
# **The stripes are the whole read, exactly as on the crop.** Six dark bands running pole to
# pole, authored with `material_arc` — an arc holds its angle for the length of the loft, and
# on a melon lofted along its own long axis that is a stripe from top to bottom. At icon size
# the stripes are two or three pixels each and they are still the only thing separating this
# from a lime, a cabbage and the green apple that is not in the game yet but will be.
#
# `sides=12` rather than the squash's 5. The squash can afford a coarse section because its
# silhouette is carried by six lobes; a melon's silhouette is a smooth curve and nothing
# else, so a coarse section reads as a cut gem — and worse, the stripes then land one per
# flat facet and become painted chevrons instead of bands wrapping a curve.
#
# Colour is the crop's rind pair, so the fruit in the bed, the drop on the ground and the
# toolbar slot are all one melon.

model watermelon
  height 0.30
  style chunky

palette
  Rind   #a9d65e
  Stripe #24521c
  Stalk  #4a7a2e

skeleton
  root base at 0.02
  # The axis of the fruit. "The melon is longer" is one `len` on this bone.
  bone core parent=base dir=up len=0.62

parts
  # The melon: a barrel, symmetric about its own half length. Every ring mirrors across
  # `0.50` deliberately — any asymmetry at all starts to read as a gourd or a pear, and the
  # one thing a watermelon is, is even at both ends.
  loft melon bones=core..core material=Rind sides=12 material_arc=Stripe:0-11,Stripe:60-71,Stripe:120-131,Stripe:180-191,Stripe:240-251,Stripe:300-311
    ring 0.00 w=0.10 d=0.10
    ring 0.10 w=0.34 d=0.34
    ring 0.30 w=0.46 d=0.46
    ring 0.50 w=0.49 d=0.49
    ring 0.70 w=0.46 d=0.46
    ring 0.90 w=0.34 d=0.34
    ring 1.00 w=0.10 d=0.10
    cap start=dome end=dome

  # The cut stalk. Same tell as the squash's peduncle and it is doing the same job twice
  # over: a fruit with a cut stalk was picked and a fruit without one is an egg, and it is
  # the only non-green thing near the top, which is what stops the melon losing its end
  # against a green-lit hand. Short — a watermelon's stalk is a stub, not a handle.
  loft stalk bone=core at=1.0 dir=up pitch=-6 len=0.10 material=Stalk sides=5
    ring 0.00 w=0.075 d=0.075
    ring 1.00 w=0.060 d=0.060
    cap start=flat end=flat

checks
  # Oblong, and in the band `ItemModels.js` can normalise honestly since it scales on the Y
  # extent alone. Rounder than this is a cabbage; longer is a marrow.
  assert height / width in 1.15..1.65
  # Round in section: a melon flattened on one axis is a squash.
  assert width / depth == 1.0 +- 0.10
  # EVEN AT BOTH ENDS. The assertion that keeps it from drifting into a pear: the widest
  # ring is the middle one, and the two poles are the same size as each other.
  assert width(melon) > 0.40
  # The stalk sits on top and is small. A stalk as fat as the fruit eats the silhouette.
  assert bottom(stalk) > top(melon) * 0.86
  assert width(stalk) < width(melon) * 0.30
  assert parts == 2
  assert tris < 900
  measure oblong height / width
